(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284250 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284251 13194139534366 true] [17592186284250 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284254 13194139534366 true] [17592186284250 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284256 13194139534366 true] [17592186284250 1089 "q-271012" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284250 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534366 true] [17592186284250 1100 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}>0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃\\,($\n{{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty )$" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284250 1113 #uuid "065744d0-6e0f-5671-bdb3-64fff73f5efa" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284250 1145 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe only solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ is $x=0.$ This root divides the number line into $2$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,0),\\,(0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284251 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284252 13194139534366 true] [17592186284251 1000 #edb/ref 1