17 #edb/ref 17592186284265 13194139534366 true] [17592186284258 1089 "q-271013" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284258 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534366 true] [17592186284258 1100 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}-39{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n$x\\in $\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]⋃\\,{$\n{{field-3}}$}⋃\\,[$\n{{field-4}}$,$\n$\\infty )$" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284258 1113 #uuid "c70bbcce-3318-5de9-9a45-73b7c5f6af37" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284258 1145 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-39{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+10x-39) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+13x-3x-39) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+13)-3(x+13)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-3)(x+13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x-3)(x+13).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-13,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-13),\\,(-13,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-13)$ | $(-13,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+13$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-13),$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-13]\\cup {0}\\cup [3,\\infty ).$" 13194139534366 true] [17592186284259 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284260 13194139534366 true] [17592186284259 1040 #edb/